es and and that's  that's why we keep showing up to these  these cops why we keep on showing up in  this space is because  even though we might not know the the  thing that will finally make change  happen we keep doing everything that we  can and we keep showing up because um  the cost of silence is too big of a  price to pay for us  and  it's entering the final days the summit  in glasgow and this is when the  decisions start to be made i think  there's um  197 involved parties in these  negotiations in the final days to get  some concrete commitments to get a time  frame to get a timetable to get a method  of reporting back so we can see how  countries make progress  what is your call to action to those  negotiators as they head into these  final days  the call to action has always been as  simple as ambitious mitigation that we  know  it is keeping fossil fuels in the ground  and and ending all exploration of these  industries that feed the climate crisis  and and moving towards a more  sustainable and renewable energy source  for our planet we know what's destroying  us we know it has to end and it really  will just take that political will to  finally get us over those those goals  that we've made for so long  and and the second ask for pacific  island in particular is around climate  financing is how do we make sure that  justice is found by the nations who are  living with the consequences of climate  change but have done so little or have  not even made like a little speck in the  the global emissions and so it is  adaptation and its mitigation and and  making sure that those goals and those  targets are ambitious are as ambitious  as we need to be  yeah that is one of the big decisions  that's being made at the end of this  summit how they control the flow of  money across borders to make sure that  developing countries and and smaller uh  islands um like yours are helped and  you started your journey as a climate  activist at the young age of 11.  and i j